46
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:30,320
And let's move on to section 3: the natural historical experiment.

47
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:35,640
Here a big problem arises for economists: the notorious reverse causality.

48
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:39,920
In practice, was it the introduction of the potato that caused the population to explode, or was it

49
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:44,560
a rapidly growing population that desperately needed to plant potatoes?

50
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:47,600
It's a bit of the classic chicken and egg dilemma.

51
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,000
To get out of it scientifically, the research uses geography and the concept of suitability

52
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,240
for agriculture, based exclusively on climate and soil data, which of course are immutable

53
00:03:57,240 --> 00:04:01,560
and do not depend on humans. The researchers obtain a totally independent variable

54
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:02,880
to run a clean test.

55
00:04:02,879 --> 00:04:05,560
No bias, no human choices involved.

56
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:10,319
A super-elegant method is thus applied, which in economics we call difference-in-

57
00:04:10,319 --> 00:04:11,319
differences.

58
00:04:11,319 --> 00:04:16,079
In simple terms, first you identify the regions perfect for growing potatoes and those

59
00:04:16,079 --> 00:04:17,399
totally unsuitable.

60
00:04:17,399 --> 00:04:23,079
Second, you look at the data before the year 1700, and third, you compare them with those after

61
00:04:23,079 --> 00:04:24,319
1700.

62
00:04:24,319 --> 00:04:30,519
In this brilliant way, we manage to surgically isolate the effect of the potato solely and exclusively

63
00:04:30,519 --> 00:04:33,120
in places where nature allowed it to thrive.

64
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:37,439
And here we are at the results, section 4: population and cities.

65
00:04:37,439 --> 00:04:41,159
And the regression models show a very clear discontinuity.

66
00:04:41,159 --> 00:04:47,079
After centuries of stagnation, from the year 1700 onwards, we see unmistakable growth.

67
00:04:47,079 --> 00:04:52,319
The data certify that population and urbanization shot up proportionally

68
00:04:52,319 --> 00:04:57,639
and specifically only in those areas that were geographically suitable for potatoes,

69
00:04:57,639 --> 00:05:00,079
coinciding precisely with its mass diffusion.

70
00:05:00,199 --> 00:05:04,159
In regions unsuitable for its cultivation, however, during the same time frame nothing

71
00:05:04,159 --> 00:05:05,959
comparable happened.

72
00:05:05,959 --> 00:05:10,240
And this brilliantly illustrates the robustness of the study.

73
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:13,399
There is a perfect and verifiable alignment.

74
00:05:13,399 --> 00:05:18,360
The nations that had a high percentage of land that was, let's say, naturally suitable

75
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:23,399
from a climatic perspective, are indeed those that dominated the global production

76
00:05:23,399 --> 00:05:26,479
of potatoes documented for the year 1900.

77
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:31,200
In practice, nature provided the perfect preconditions and economic history

78
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:32,800
seized the opportunity.

79
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:38,120
But get ready, because the crucial point of the whole investigation is this number.

80
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:41,879
According to estimates based on truly rigorous mathematical models,

81
00:05:41,879 --> 00:05:46,520
